---
import manifest from '../../content/images.json';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ArrowUpRight, CloudRain, Snowflake, Thermometer } from '@lucide/astro';
---
<Layout title="El Niño & La Niña U.S. Winter Impacts: Regional Guides" active="winter" description="Understand broad El Niño and La Niña U.S. winter tendencies, then compare California rain and snow, Pacific Northwest snowpack and the dated CPC winter outlook.">
  <div class="page-shell subpage lifecycle-page">
    <div class="page-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">REGIONAL ENSO GUIDES · REVIEWED NOVEMBER 21, 2026</span><h1>El Niño &amp; La Niña<br /><span>U.S. winter impacts.</span></h1><p>ENSO shifts broad seasonal odds, not a local forecast. Start with a regional tendency, then check the season, elevation and official forecast that matter to your decision.</p></div></div>
    <section class="regional-section" aria-labelledby="guides-heading"><h2 id="guides-heading">Choose your region or question</h2><div class="regional-grid">
      <article><img class="card-preview" src={manifest.assets['sierra-snow-level'].src} width={manifest.assets['sierra-snow-level'].width} height={manifest.assets['sierra-snow-level'].height} alt={manifest.assets['sierra-snow-level'].alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><CloudRain size={24} /><h3>California rain or Sierra snow?</h3><p>A wetter outlook cannot determine the snow line. Separate storm precipitation, resort snowfall and water stored in the snowpack.</p><a href="/impacts/california-snow-vs-rain/">California rain and snow <ArrowUpRight size={13} /></a></article>
      <article><img class="card-preview" src={manifest.assets['northwest-snow'].src} width={manifest.assets['northwest-snow'].width} height={manifest.assets['northwest-snow'].height} alt={manifest.assets['northwest-snow'].alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><p class="image-credit">USDA Forest Service / Todd Leeds; Yellow Pine, Idaho. Location photo, not current snow conditions. <a href={manifest.assets['northwest-snow'].source}>Photo source</a>.</p><Snowflake size={24} /><h3>Pacific Northwest winters</h3><p>How El Niño and La Niña can shift winter conditions in Washington, Oregon and Idaho, and why mountain snow differs from city snow.</p><a href="/impacts/pacific-northwest-winter/">Northwest winter and snowpack <ArrowUpRight size={13} /></a></article>
      <article><img class="card-preview" src={manifest.assets['winter-temperature'].src} width={manifest.assets['winter-temperature'].width} height={manifest.assets['winter-temperature'].height} alt={manifest.assets['winter-temperature'].alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><p class="image-credit">NOAA CPC · issued November 19, 2026 · DJF 2026–27. <a href="/winter-outlook/">Map legend and interpretation</a>.</p><Thermometer size={24} /><h3>U.S. winter 2026–27</h3><p>The saved November 19, 2026 CPC maps for December–February, with category probabilities and regional interpretation.</p><a href="/winter-outlook/">Dated U.S. winter outlook <ArrowUpRight size={13} /></a></article>
    </div></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="current-winter-heading"><h2 id="current-winter-heading">Winter 2026–27: use the November issue</h2><p class="body-copy">The November 19 CPC outlook for December–February increases wetter odds for California, the Desert Southwest and the East Coast compared with October. Across the Northwest, southwestern Oregon favors wetter conditions while Washington and northern Oregon have equal chances for precipitation. These are dated seasonal categories, separate from the historical phase tendencies below.</p><p class="body-copy">See <a href="/winter-outlook/#november-changes-heading">what changed in the November outlook</a> and <a href="/learn/el-nino-and-drought-recovery/">why wetter odds do not guarantee drought recovery</a>. Seasonal probabilities cannot determine storm timing, local snowfall or flood warnings.</p></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section"><h2>How do U.S. winter tendencies compare?</h2><p class="body-copy">These are broad late-fall and winter relationships described by the National Weather Service, not a forecast for a particular winter. The relationship varies by region and event; La Niña is not an exact mirror of El Niño.</p>
      <div class="probability-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Broad U.S. ENSO winter tendencies" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table"><caption>Historical tendencies · no local numerical probabilities assigned</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Region</th><th scope="col">El Niño tendency</th><th scope="col">La Niña tendency</th><th scope="col">What still needs a forecast?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Pacific Northwest</th><td>Warmer; often drier</td><td>Cooler; can be wetter</td><td>Snow levels, storm totals and runoff</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Northern U.S.</th><td>Warmer seasonal conditions</td><td>Cooler seasonal conditions</td><td>Cold outbreaks and individual snowstorms</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Southern U.S.</th><td>More active southern storm track; wetter tendency</td><td>Drier and warmer tendency</td><td>Where and when storms occur</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <p class="body-copy">An ENSO phase does not settle every regional outlook. In a specific issue, CPC may favor above-, near- or below-normal conditions, or show equal chances. Use the dated regional product rather than treating this table as a replacement.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="explanation-grid"><div><h2>Neutral is a third state.</h2><p>ENSO-neutral does not promise near-normal local weather. Other drivers and long-term trends still matter, and regional outlooks can favor a category during neutral conditions.</p><a href="/forecast/enso-neutral/">What ENSO-neutral means</a></div><div><h2>A phase is not a warning.</h2><p>Flooding, heavy snow and dangerous cold can occur in different ENSO phases. Use current National Weather Service forecasts and warnings for travel and safety decisions.</p><a href="https://www.weather.gov/">Local forecasts and warnings</a></div></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section"><h2>Sources and scope</h2><p class="body-copy">Sources checked November 12, 2026: <a href="https://www.weather.gov/mhx/ensowhat">NWS ENSO definitions and typical winter circulation</a>; USDA Northwest Climate Hub guides to <a href="https://www.climatehubs.usda.gov/hubs/northwest/topic/el-nino-northwest-what-can-we-expect">El Niño</a> and <a href="https://www.climatehubs.usda.gov/hubs/northwest/topic/la-nina-northwest-what-can-we-expect">La Niña</a>. This index focuses on U.S. winter questions. It does not provide a global hazard forecast. For current ENSO state probabilities, use the <a href="/forecast/">dated CPC forecast</a>.</p></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="regional-reading-heading"><h2 id="regional-reading-heading">A three-question regional reading</h2><div class="explanation-grid"><div><h3>What is the season?</h3><p>Match the regional tendency to its valid months. A winter relationship does not describe summer, and overlapping seasons are not separate monthly forecasts.</p></div><div><h3>What is the impact variable?</h3><p>Temperature, precipitation, snowpack and water supply respond on different timescales. A wetter season is not automatically a deeper snowpack or a recovered reservoir.</p></div><div><h3>What is the decision horizon?</h3><p>Use the dated seasonal map for broad odds, then switch to local forecasts and warnings as a storm, trip or safety decision approaches.</p></div></div><p class="body-copy">This workflow keeps the broad ENSO tendency useful without turning it into a city-level promise. Start with the <a href="/winter-outlook/">dated winter outlook</a> when the target is DJF 2026–27.</p></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section"><h2>Explore the science</h2><p class="body-copy"><a href="/learn/el-nino-and-drought-recovery/">Can El Niño end a drought? Rainfall versus recovery</a> · <a href="/learn/">All ENSO learning guides</a></p></section>
  </div>
</Layout>
<style>
  .image-credit { font-size:12px; line-height:1.7; color:var(--muted); }
  .image-credit a { margin-top:0; }
  .card-preview { display:block; width:100%; height:auto; aspect-ratio:5 / 3; object-fit:contain; background:#10201d; margin-bottom:20px; border-radius:8px; }
  .regional-grid a, .explanation-grid a { display: inline-block; margin-top: 14px; color: var(--green); font-size: 13px; }
  #current-winter-heading { scroll-margin-top: 110px; }
  table { min-width: 740px; }
  th, td { white-space: normal; min-width: 150px; }
</style>
